# the claims of mill.md as claude-opus-5-5 read them: check that they say what the text says
# Comment: "desirable" changes its sense. In S3, drawn by analogy with "visible" and "audible", it means
# capable of being desired. In S5-S6 it means worth desiring, a good. Each sense has its own atom.
# The modal phrasings ("capable of being given", "possible to produce", "could ever convince", "can be given")
# are folded into the atom wording rather than written with [] and <>, so the general claims can be quantified.
atom	proven_visible(x)	proof can be given that x is visible
atom	seen(x)	people actually see x
atom	proven_audible(x)	proof can be given that x is audible
atom	heard(x)	people hear x
atom	proven_desirable_cap(x)	evidence can be produced that x is desirable, in the sense of capable of being desired, by analogy with visible and audible
atom	desired(x)	people do actually desire x
atom	acknowledged_end(x)	x is acknowledged, in theory and in practice, to be an end
atom	convincible_end(x)	some person could be convinced that x is an end
atom	reason_desirable_good(x)	a reason can be given why x is desirable, in the sense of worth desiring
atom	person(x)	x is a person
atom	desires_own_happiness(x)	x desires his own happiness, so far as he believes it attainable
atom	full_proof_good(x)	we have all the proof the case admits of, and all it is possible to require, that x is a good
atom	good(x)	x is a good
atom	own_happiness_good(x)	x's happiness is a good to x
atom	good_to_aggregate(x)	x is a good to the aggregate of all persons
atom	end_of_conduct(x)	x has made out its title as one of the ends of conduct
atom	criterion_of_morality(x)	x is one of the criteria of morality
# Individuals: h is happiness, the end the utilitarian doctrine proposes to itself; gh is the general happiness.
domain	h gh
P1	S1	∀x (proven_visible(x) → seen(x))
P2	S2	∀x (proven_audible(x) → heard(x))
P3	S3	∀x (proven_desirable_cap(x) → desired(x))
P4	S4	¬acknowledged_end(h) → ¬convincible_end(h)
P5	S5	reason_desirable_good(gh) → ∀x (person(x) → desires_own_happiness(x))
P6	S6	∀x (person(x) → desires_own_happiness(x))	kind empirical
P7	S6	∀x (person(x) → desires_own_happiness(x)) → full_proof_good(h)
P8	S6	good(h)
P9	S6	∀x (person(x) → own_happiness_good(x))
P10	S6	∀x (person(x) → own_happiness_good(x)) → good_to_aggregate(gh)
P11	S6	good_to_aggregate(gh)
P12	S7	end_of_conduct(h)
P13	S7	end_of_conduct(h) → criterion_of_morality(h)
P14	S7	criterion_of_morality(h)
# read by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/claims 088c436c8b72, 2026-10-19, blinded by name space
